{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Behold, to HaShem your Elohim belong the heavens and the highest heavens, the earth and all that is in it.</b> It appears to me here that if Scripture had wished to exalt Israel above all the other nations, it would have sufficed to say: 'Behold, to HaShem your Elohim belong the earth and all that is in it — only in your fathers did He delight,' and there would have been no need to mention the heavens and the highest heavens. But it is possible to say that it comes to hint that this lower world is the core of all existence, and the verse is full of it — (Genesis 1:15): ״יהי מאורות ברקיע השמים להאיר על הארץ״ — which explains that the spheres, despite being created enormous, the intention in them is for the needs of this species, and even though the philosophers of inquiry refute this, we have nothing but the way of our Torah and the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said: 'The righteous are greater than the ministering angels.' And so we find that David, of blessed memory, mentioned (Psalms 148:13–14): ״הודו על ארץ ושמים״ and ״וירם קרן לעמו״ — he says that the Holy One, blessed be He, raised the horn of Israel above all the created beings that are in the heavens and on earth. And it says further (Psalms 103): ״ברכו ה' מלאכיו״ and so on, ״ברכו ה' כל צבאיו״ and so on, ״ברכו ה' כל מעשיו״ — he mentioned the three worlds from above to below, and concerning the lower world he said ״כל מעשיו״ — all His works — meaning that it encompasses all His works. And they said in the Midrash on (Joel 2:11): ״כי רב מאד מחנהו״ — these are the ministering angels; ״וכי עצום עושי דברו״ — these are Israel. And furthermore we have proofs from what the philosophers asked: why does the sphere revolve constantly in that same revolution — has it attained or has it not attained? If it has attained, then it revolves in vain; if it has not attained, what benefit is there in its revolution? For one cannot say that it adds to its attainment day by day, for if so it should have been renewing and adding to the benefits of this world, which is sustained by its motion. And they answered: the reason for its revolution is in order to sustain the lower world, for through its motion the four elements that are below on earth are combined, without which the world cannot endure. See, this is a proof that the philosophers of inquiry concede that the existence of the spheres is for the sake of man, and they are the ones who say that the spheres have no purpose except to govern the nature of existence." "<b>And if</b> you say: this is possible regarding the seven wandering stars [planets], which govern this world, and they are what were created for that purpose — but the eighth and ninth are supreme, pure in themselves, and were not created for the use of this world. Your answer is that all nine were created for the sake of this world, and all of them need one another, and the motion of one is on account of its companion, from the lowest to the highest, for all of them are within one another like onion layers [כגלדי בצלים]. And it has already been clarified in the books of wisdom that all of existence is one, from the uppermost sphere down to the point called the center of the world, or the pillar of the world. And we have received a proof for this in the word אחד: for the letter אל״ף alludes to the uppermost encompassing sphere called ערבות, which is unique among all the spheres like a king over them; the letter ח׳ alludes to the eight spheres that are encompassed within it; the letter ד׳ alludes to the four elements beneath them; and all is אחד — one. And just as it is one, so its Governor, blessed be He, is one. And behold, nine spheres and four elements are 13, like the numerical value of אחד. And what it says — 'Behold, to HaShem your Elohim belong the heavens and the highest heavens' — השמים are the spheres, and שמי השמים are the elements, which are by the power of the heavens beneath them, in the manner of 'the earth and all that is in it.' And so it says in the psalm (Psalms 148:1): ״הללו את ה' מן השמים״, and it mentions His angels and His hosts and the stars of light, and afterward ״הללוהו שמי השמים״. This is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, on the expression 'the heavens,' whose meaning is the spheres and the elements that are beneath them." "<b>But</b> the Rambam, of blessed memory, explained that שמי השמים refers to the nine spheres, and said that השמים is the encompassing sphere and שמי השמים are those within it in which the stars are. And since all the spheres are included in these two parts — one part that has no stars at all, and a second part together with stars — therefore Scripture made two parts here and said שמי השמים. And so in tractate Ḥagigah, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, divided them into two parts — that is, what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned there: Resh Lakish said, 'There are two firmaments,' as it is said: ״הן לה' אלהיך השמים ושמי השמים״. This is the opinion of the Rav, of blessed memory, in the Guide for the Perplexed regarding the expression 'the heavens and the highest heavens.'" "<b>And you should be roused</b> to reflect on the matter of R. Yehoshua [or: R. Yehudah] who says, 'There are two firmaments,' for corresponding to them were the two pieces of wood on the altar-arrangement [מערכה]. And that is what they said in tractate Yoma, chapter 'Amar Lahem HaMemuneh': 'The first arrangement precedes the second arrangement; the second arrangement precedes the two pieces of wood; the two pieces of wood precede the removal of the ashes from the inner altar.' The first arrangement alludes to the entire structure; the second arrangement to the existence of the angels; the two pieces of wood allude to all the spheres, which are divided into these two parts, from which the decrees come to the lower world; and the removal of ashes from the inner altar alludes to the lower world. And would that we knew the rest of the matters mentioned in that baraita — what the order was among them, and what their meaning is, and what each and every one alludes to. For there is no service, of all the services performed in the Temple, that is light, that does not have a great principle behind it and that does not contain an allusion and a depiction of matters above and a model of them." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Behold, to HaShem your Elohim belong the heavens and the highest heavens — only in your fathers did HaShem delight' — this alludes to the souls of the fathers, which emanate from the fathers, and therefore they were a chariot [מרכבה] in their death, as if they and they are one thing. And for this reason is the delight and the attachment and the choice in their seed, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "דעת הרב ז״ל — 'the Rav' here refers to the Rambam, author of the Guide for the Perplexed (Moreh ha-Nevukhim), as the immediately preceding context establishes.", 3 "ר״י is an abbreviation; left as R. Yehoshua/R. Yehudah without resolving, as the Talmudic context in Ḥagigah (12b) cites Resh Lakish (cited in comment [2]), and the precise sage abbreviated here is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:37.739939526Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}